Riri — Complete End-to-End Thinking Process
1. The two-layer "brain" — this is the key design idea
Riri isn't one LLM doing everything. There are two distinct thinking systems working together, and understanding why is the key to the whole design:

A fast, deterministic regex/rule layer (_parse_command in dsm_controller.py) handles every phrasing the team actually uses — "skip", "start DSM", "mark X leave", "who's next", etc. Zero latency, zero LLM call.
The LLM is only invoked for things regex genuinely can't decide: extracting a task from free-form speech, classifying truly ambiguous interruptions, and — most importantly — the Task Sense Check (deciding if a transcript is real).
This matters because a live meeting can't afford an LLM round-trip on every single utterance. The regex layer catches the 90% of routine turns instantly; the LLM is reserved for judgment calls.

2. Capture → transcript (per utterance)

mic → Silero VAD (start/stop, 32ms frames) → trim silence → normalize peak → reject if too short/quiet
    → faster-whisper (biased by a vocabulary "initial_prompt") → filler-word cleanup
The vocabulary biasing (vocab.py) is a small but real trick: config/dsm_vocabulary.txt holds real names/aliases and project jargon (mined from actual past standups — "AIOS", "Confluence", "SF+", etc). This gets joined into one sentence and handed to Whisper as initial_prompt — Whisper treats it as prior context, so it's statistically biased toward hearing "AIOS" instead of "AI OS," or the right project name instead of a phonetically-similar wrong word. This is why STT accuracy on your specific team's vocabulary is better than generic Whisper.

Confidence banding comes straight from Whisper's own avg_logprob per segment (High/Medium/Low thresholds in config) — not a separate model, just Whisper's own certainty about what it heard.

3. Every utterance gets classified before it's acted on
This is the part you specifically asked about — "does Riri think about whether input is real." The answer is: classification happens in two stages, and neither stage blindly trusts what STT produced.

Stage 1 — intent classification (_classify): before Riri decides whether something is a task, a command, a question, or chat, _parse_command runs against the entire transcript. Critically, the ordering here is deliberate and encodes real product decisions:

Questions are checked before actions — so "what did you save for Mayank?" can't accidentally get parsed as a correction to Mayank's record.
Status disputes are checked before plain leave/pending matchers — so "Abhishek is not pending" doesn't literally set the thing it's denying.
If nothing matches and the phase is "chat" near a garbled "dsm"-ish word, or STT confidence was Low, it asks rather than assumes ("Did you want me to start DSM?"). It never guesses on ambiguous input.
Only if this fast layer truly can't classify something (mostly barge-in interruptions or final-review free text) does it fall through to _classify_freeform, a single LLM call.
Stage 2 — Task Sense Check (task_validator.py) — this is the deepest "is this real" reasoning in the whole system, and it's the only path by which spoken text ever becomes a saved task. Even after STT produces a clean transcript, it still isn't trusted at face value. The LLM is handed:

the raw AND cleaned transcript,
this specific employee's own recent task patterns (from dsm_task_memory.json, mined from two months of real standups),
common team tasks, known project terms, common verbs/phrases, and a literal list of "noise words that aren't real tasks",
and is asked to reason through 5 explicit steps (does this sound like a real task → does it match this team's patterns → strip noise words → normalize into a clean phrase → decide confidence), producing one of four verdicts:

Decision	Meaning
VALID_HIGH	Clean, unambiguous real task → save immediately
VALID_MEDIUM	Real task after removing some noise/minor ambiguity → save immediately
NEEDS_CONFIRMATION	Plausible but not certain → Riri reads it back and asks "did you mean X?"
INVALID	Doesn't sound like a real task at all → ask to repeat, never saved
The prompt explicitly forbids two failure modes in both directions: "Do NOT blindly trust STT confidence — even if confidence is High, if the transcript doesn't sound like a real task, don't mark it valid" and "Do NOT invent or hallucinate a task the transcript doesn't support." So a garbled-but-real task and a clean-but-nonsensical transcript are both caught.

On top of the LLM's own verdict, two deterministic safety rules override it no matter what the LLM says — these aren't LLM judgment, they're hard code:

If STT confidence was Low, any VALID_* verdict is force-downgraded to NEEDS_CONFIRMATION — a shaky transcription is a risk regardless of how clean the cleaned version reads.
If the LLM's response is malformed/unparsable JSON, or claims valid with no actual task text, it falls back to NEEDS_CONFIRMATION with a plain readback — never treated as evidence to silently save.
This is genuinely "does Riri think about whether this is real" implemented as actual code, not just a prompt instruction hoped-for by the LLM.

4. Name resolution — same paranoid pattern
names.py never trusts a fuzzy match if it could be wrong in a costly way. Two concrete guards:

A stopword list of command/filler words that can never become a name-match, so "who's name did you call?" doesn't fuzzy-match someone named "Aman".
Stricter matching thresholds for short names (≤4 letters use 0.90 similarity vs 0.78 normally) — "Son" is one edit away from "sun"/"song"/"soon", so loose matching there would invent attendance for nobody.
When two roster members share a first name (Abhishek M / Abhishek P), a bare "Abhishek" is treated as genuinely ambiguous and declined unless a human curated it as an alias in employees.json — the system fails closed rather than guessing which Abhishek.
5. Personality — deliberately not LLM-generated for routine lines
This is a specific, intentional tradeoff worth understanding: Riri's actual spoken lines during a normal meeting are not written by the LLM. persona.py has static phrase banks (multiple variants per situation, chosen randomly for variety) for every routine moment — calling someone, confirming a task, marking pending, closing the meeting. The LLM is only used for the two genuinely open-ended cases: casual chat replies and correction/task-extraction judgment calls.

Why: an LLM call per line would add real latency to every single turn of the meeting. Pre-written variants keep the meeting fast while still not sounding robotic (multiple phrasings + a throttled joke system — jokes are capped to roughly once every 4 people, chosen with a 60% chance even when eligible, and structurally forbidden on Leave/Pending/Review-required lines since the persona rules explicitly say accuracy and appropriateness come before humor).

config/riri_system_prompt.txt (~490 lines) is the actual single source of truth for identity, DSM flow, status definitions, and the full intent taxonomy — but it's not sent whole on every call. persona.load_persona_sections() splits it on its ━━━ HEADER ━━━ blocks, so e.g. task extraction only receives the "TASK UPDATE HANDLING" section, not the whole document — same source of truth, without the token/latency cost of resending 490 lines every turn.

6. State machine — where "chat" vs "DSM" mode actually lives

chat_idle → asking_to_start_dsm → dsm_greeting → calling_employee → waiting_for_task → ... → completed
                                                        │                  │
                                                handling_command    correcting_task
                                                        │
                                                     paused
self.mode is "chat" or "dsm"; self.state is one of 10 named states. The app always opens in chat mode — it does not auto-launch into a meeting. It only transitions to dsm_greeting after an explicit confirmation ("start DSM", "let's start", "go ahead") — and per the classify logic above, a garbled or low-confidence utterance near "dsm" gets a clarifying question instead of triggering the meeting, since accidentally starting a real meeting on a mishear would be a bad failure mode.

7. Output → speech
Once Riri decides what to say (either a persona phrase-bank line, or an LLM chat reply), tts.speak():

Checks the on-disk cache (keyed by voice+text hash) — this is why routine lines (all pre-warmed at startup) play back near-instantly instead of paying Kokoro synthesis latency every time.
Synthesizes via Kokoro if not cached, plays via sounddevice, routed to whichever output device is configured (default speakers, or VB-Cable when in Teams mode).
Barge-in is currently disabled on your machine (measured directly: Riri's own voice saturates VAD at probability 1.0 with no AEC, indistinguishable from real speech at any threshold) — so in practice she always finishes her sentence before listening again.
8. Teams routing — why two separate virtual devices, not one
Worth being precise about why two different drivers, since it's not obvious:

VB-Cable solves one direction only: Riri's TTS → a virtual "microphone" Teams can select. Writing audio to VB-Cable's output is literally the same pipe as reading it back on VB-Cable's input (on macOS it's one combined device; on Windows it's exposed as two separately-named devices, "CABLE Input"/"CABLE Output" — same underlying mechanism, different naming).
BlackHole solves the other direction: letting Riri hear Teams' participants. macOS's CoreAudio has no OS-level loopback API at all (unlike Windows' WASAPI, which the original Windows-only code used) — so instead, a macOS Multi-Output Device (built in Audio MIDI Setup) is used to physically fan Teams' call audio out to both your real speakers and BlackHole simultaneously. Riri then just reads BlackHole as a completely ordinary input device — no loopback trickery needed on macOS at all, which is actually architecturally simpler than the Windows path once BlackHole is in the graph.
That's the full pipeline, from the physical air pressure hitting the mic to the physical air pressure leaving the speaker, including every point where the system deliberately asks itself "is this real / am I sure" rather than just trusting the previous stage.

