How Riri Actually Thinks, Start to Finish
The big idea first
Riri isn't one AI brain doing everything. It's mostly a fast rule-follower, with a real thinking model (the LLM) only called in for the handful of moments that genuinely need judgment. This matters because if every single sentence had to go through a full AI "think about this" step, the meeting would feel painfully slow. So most of what Riri does is instant pattern-matching, and only the hard calls get escalated to actual reasoning.

Step 1: Turning your voice into text
Your voice hits the mic, and before anything else happens, a small detector called VAD figures out "is someone actually talking right now, or is this silence/background noise." It watches in tiny 32-millisecond slices, waits for real speech to start, and waits for a proper pause before deciding you're done — that's what stops it from chopping off the first word of your sentence or cutting you off mid-thought.

Once it has a full chunk of speech, it hands that to the transcription engine (Whisper), which turns it into text. One nice trick here: before transcribing, Riri quietly feeds it a list of your team's actual names and jargon — "Aakanksha," "AIOS," "Confluence," "SF+," whatever's specific to your team. This biases it toward hearing those correctly instead of guessing something that merely sounds similar. Whisper also tells us how confident it is in what it heard — that confidence score matters a lot later.

Step 2: "What did they actually mean?"
Now Riri has some text. Before doing anything with it, she has to figure out what kind of thing you just said — a question, a command, a task update, small talk, a correction, whatever. This is where the fast rule-based layer does most of the work: it checks the sentence against a big list of known phrasings ("skip", "mark someone on leave", "who's next," etc.) and if it clearly matches one, that's instant — no AI thinking needed, she just knows what you meant.

A few of these rules are deliberately ordered to avoid dumb mistakes:

If your sentence is a question ("what did you save for Mayank?"), that gets caught before it could be mistaken for you trying to correct Mayank's task.
If you're disputing something ("Abhishek is NOT pending"), that's checked before the plain "mark as pending" rule — otherwise the negation would get ignored and it'd do the opposite of what you said.
If she's not sure whether you're trying to start the meeting (garbled speech, or low confidence in what she heard), she doesn't guess — she just asks "did you want me to start DSM?" rather than risk starting a real meeting on a mishearing.
Only when none of these rules match — usually when you interrupt her mid-sentence with something unpredictable — does she actually hand the sentence to the AI and ask it to figure out what you meant. That's the expensive path, used sparingly on purpose.

Step 3: The real "is this actually true / real" check
This is the part you're most interested in, and it's the deepest thinking Riri does. Even after she has a clean transcript of your task update, she does not just trust it and save it. Every single task update — no matter how it was said — goes through one dedicated sanity check before it's ever recorded.

Here's what that check actually does: it hands the AI your raw and cleaned-up sentence, plus some context about your team — things you personally have said before in past standups, common tasks the team reports, known project terms, and even a list of words that are usually just noise and not real work ("rabbit," "lion," random junk that sometimes sneaks into a transcription). Then it asks the AI to reason through it step by step: does this sound like a real task? Does it match the kind of thing this person usually says? Strip out anything that's obviously noise. Then decide how confident it is.

The AI comes back with one of four verdicts:

Clearly a real task, no cleanup needed → save it right away.
Real task, but had to clean up some noise or minor confusion → still save it right away.
Might be a real task, but not sure enough → she reads it back to you and asks "did you mean X?" before saving anything.
Doesn't sound like a real task at all → nothing gets saved, she just asks you to repeat yourself.
And there are two hard rules that override the AI's own judgment no matter what it says, because relying purely on the AI's confidence isn't safe enough on its own:

If the transcription itself was low-confidence (Whisper wasn't sure what it heard), she always double-checks with you first — even if the cleaned-up version reads perfectly, a shaky transcription is still a real risk of having heard something wrong.
If the AI's response comes back broken, malformed, or claims something is valid but gives no actual task text — none of that ever counts as permission to save. It always falls back to asking you rather than guessing.
So the honest answer to "does Riri check if this is real" is: yes, genuinely, and it's not just one AI call blindly trusted — it's an AI judgment wrapped in hard safety rules that can override it.

Step 4: Matching names correctly
When someone's name comes up in a sentence, Riri has to match what she heard to someone on the actual team list — and mishearing a name wrong could genuinely mark the wrong person absent, which is a real problem, not a cosmetic one. So the matching is deliberately cautious:

Common filler/command words are blocked from ever being mistaken for a name — so "who's name did you call?" doesn't accidentally get matched to someone named "Aman."
Short names get a much stricter matching bar, because a short name like "Son" is dangerously close to ordinary words like "sun" or "soon" — loose matching there would invent someone's turn out of nowhere.
If two people share a first name and she genuinely can't tell which one you meant, she doesn't guess — she treats it as ambiguous and asks, unless someone already told her in advance which one that name usually refers to.
Step 5: How she decides what to actually say
Here's a deliberate design choice: for the routine, repetitive parts of a meeting — calling someone's name, confirming a task, marking someone pending, closing the meeting — Riri is not making that up fresh with the AI each time. Those lines are pre-written (several variants of each, picked at random so it doesn't feel robotic and repetitive), because asking the AI to invent a fresh sentence for every single routine moment would add real delay to every turn of the meeting. The AI is only actually writing text when you're chatting casually with her, or when something needs real judgment (like the task-checking step above).

There's also a small joke system layered on top of the routine lines — she'll occasionally add a light joke after confirming a task, but never every time, never too often, and never on serious moments like marking someone absent or unclear — accuracy and appropriateness always come first.

Step 6: Chat mode vs. meeting mode
She always starts in casual chat mode, not straight into a meeting — she'll greet you and ask if you want to start today's standup. She only switches into actual meeting mode once you clearly confirm it. And again, if your confirmation was garbled or she wasn't confident in what she heard, she asks instead of just starting the meeting on a guess, since accidentally kicking off a real meeting because of a mishearing would be a bad mistake.

Step 7: Turning her reply into speech
Once she's decided what to say, it gets spoken out loud. For anything she's said before in this session (which covers most of the routine lines, since they're all pre-generated once at startup), playback is instant because it's already been recorded — she's not re-generating the audio every single time. For anything new (real chat replies), it does need to be generated fresh, which takes a little longer.

One current limitation, worth knowing plainly: on this Mac, she can't tell the difference between "the user is interrupting me" and "my own voice bounced back through the microphone" — so interrupting her mid-sentence right now doesn't actually work reliably. She'll always finish what she's saying before listening again.

Step 8: Getting her voice into a Teams call
Two separate pieces of software are doing two separate jobs here, which is easy to get confused:

One virtual device (VB-Cable) lets her speak into the meeting — whatever she says gets piped into something Teams treats as your microphone.
A second piece (BlackHole, plus a small setup step combining it with your speakers) lets her hear the meeting — Mac doesn't have a built-in way to "listen to whatever's currently playing," so this routes the meeting's audio to both your ears and to something Riri can listen in on, at the same time.
That's the complete path — from the raw sound hitting the microphone, through every point where Riri is genuinely checking "am I sure about this, or should I ask instead of guessing," all the way to the sound coming out the other end.

