Riri's Flow — With the Actual Tools Marked, and Why Each Decision Was Made
Step 1: Voice → Text
Tools used: sounddevice (captures raw mic audio) → Silero VAD (decides when you start/stop talking) → faster-whisper large-v3-turbo (turns speech into text)

The constraint that shaped this: Everything here has to run on your Mac's CPU, in real time, while also running a whole language model at the same time. There's no cloud service doing the heavy lifting — no internet round-trip, but also no borrowed processing power. On a machine this constrained, the choice of which Whisper model matters a lot:

We picked large-v3-turbo instead of the biggest, most accurate large-v3 specifically because it's noticeably faster while losing only a small amount of accuracy — the README's own benchmark showed roughly 3 seconds vs 4 seconds transcribing the same clip on this machine's CPU. In a live meeting, every second of lag between "you stopped talking" and "Riri responds" is felt directly, so we leaned toward speed. The larger model is still available as a manual override (STT_HIGH_ACCURACY=1) for a noisy room or a name-heavy meeting, but it's not the default because most of the time it's not worth the extra wait.
The VAD (Silero) exists at all because without it, Riri would either need you to press a button to say "I'm done talking," or she'd have to guess based on a fixed timer — both worse. But VAD running locally, in real time, alongside everything else, is itself a CPU cost, so it's a genuinely light, small model rather than a heavier alternative — it needs to be cheap enough to run continuously without competing for resources with the LLM.
Step 2: Deciding what you meant
Tools used: mostly plain code (pattern matching against known phrasings) — no AI model for this step, except in rare fallback cases where it calls the local LLM (Qwen3-4B)

The constraint that shaped this: this is the single biggest performance decision in the whole system, and it's directly downstream of running the LLM locally instead of in the cloud.

Here's the real problem: a cloud AI service (like the DeepSeek API this project was originally built around) responds in maybe half a second to a second, because it's running on serious dedicated hardware somewhere else. A 4-billion-parameter model running locally on a laptop's chip is dramatically slower per request than that, even with Apple's MLX optimization. If every single sentence in the meeting — "skip", "who's next", "mark him on leave", a plain task answer — had to go through a full LLM call, the meeting would feel sluggish and unnatural; you'd be waiting a beat after every sentence for the AI to "think," even for things that don't need thinking at all.

So the decision was: handle everything predictable with instant, zero-cost pattern matching, and only escalate to the actual LLM for the small number of situations that genuinely can't be handled that way (mainly: interpreting an unpredictable mid-sentence interruption). This is a direct workaround for local-model latency — if this were still running against a fast cloud API, this two-tier system would matter far less, because even the "just ask the AI every time" approach would feel acceptably fast.

Step 3: The "is this actually real" Task Sense Check
Tools used: the local LLM (Qwen3-4B) — same model as everywhere else, just asked a different kind of question

The constraint that shaped this: this is one of the few places where we do pay the cost of a real LLM call on every single task update, and that was a deliberate decision, not an oversight. Task accuracy matters more here than speed, because a wrongly-saved task is a worse outcome than an extra second of latency — this is the one place slowness is an acceptable price. Everywhere else in the system, we designed around avoiding LLM calls specifically because local models are slow; here we accepted the slowness because getting it wrong (saving garbage as someone's real task, or losing a real task because of noise) is worse than a short delay.

The hard safety rules on top of this (never trust a VALID_* verdict if the transcription itself was low-confidence, never trust a malformed AI response) exist for a related reason: a local 4B model is meaningfully less capable than a large cloud model. It's good, but it's not as robust — malformed JSON responses, questionable judgment calls, etc. are more likely with a smaller local model than with the biggest cloud models. Wrapping it in deterministic override rules is a direct compensation for choosing a small, fast, locally-runnable model over a bigger, more expensive cloud one.

Step 4: Name matching
Tools used: plain Python text comparison (difflib) — no AI at all

The constraint that shaped this: this one isn't really about speed — it's about reliability. An AI model, even a good one, can occasionally get creative or inconsistent. Matching a person's name to the correct team member is exactly the kind of decision where you want the same input to always produce the same output, with zero risk of the AI "interpreting" a name into someone else. So this was kept as boring, predictable code specifically to avoid AI unpredictability in a spot where a mistake (marking the wrong person absent) is a real, tangible error, not just an awkward phrasing.

Step 5: Deciding what Riri actually says
Tools used: pre-written phrase banks (plain text, no AI) for routine lines, plus the local LLM (Qwen3-4B) for open-ended chat and judgment calls; final spoken audio through Kokoro TTS

The constraint that shaped this: same local-speed problem as Step 2. Generating a brand-new sentence with an LLM for every single routine moment ("thanks, moving to the next person") would mean paying LLM latency on literally every turn, all meeting long. So instead, those lines are written once, with a few random variations, and reused — genuinely zero latency, since there's no model call at all. The LLM is reserved for the moments that actually need it: free-flowing chat before the meeting starts, and task-related judgment calls.

There's a second local-performance trick layered on top: the TTS caching system. Every one of those routine phrase-bank lines gets synthesized into actual audio once, ahead of time, right when Riri starts up (this is the "warming the cache" step you may have seen scroll by). After that, replaying any of those lines is close to instant — reading a pre-made audio file instead of generating speech from scratch. This exists because speech synthesis, like everything else running locally, isn't instantaneous — doing it live for a repeated phrase like "got it, thanks" every single time would be wasted work when the exact same audio was already generated once before.

Step 6: The mic-echo / interruption problem
Constraint, not really a tool decision: this one isn't about speed at all — it's a hardware limitation. Interrupting Riri mid-sentence requires the system to tell the difference between "the user is talking" and "Riri's own voice, played through the speaker, is bouncing back into the microphone." Professional video-call software solves this with something called acoustic echo cancellation (AEC) — dedicated logic that subtracts the known output signal from the input signal in real time. This Mac's setup doesn't have that wired in for this use case, and we measured it directly: Riri's own voice registers as "100% definitely speech" to the detector, completely indistinguishable from a real interruption. No amount of adjusting sensitivity fixes that, because the signal genuinely looks identical either way. So the decision was to just turn interruption-detection off rather than have it constantly misfire — a real capability we're giving up, but the alternative (constant false interruptions) was worse than no interruptions at all.

Step 7: Getting Riri into a Microsoft Teams call
Tools used: VB-Cable (lets her voice be treated as a microphone) and BlackHole + Mac's built-in Audio MIDI Setup (lets her hear the call)

The constraint that shaped this: this software was originally built for Windows, which has a built-in feature (called WASAPI loopback) letting an app directly "listen to whatever is currently playing" with no extra tools. Macs simply don't have that feature at the operating-system level — it doesn't exist here, full stop. So on Windows, hearing the call needed zero extra software; on this Mac, we had to install a second virtual device (BlackHole) and manually build a small audio-routing setup to recreate the same capability that Windows gives for free. That's not a design choice we made for style — it's a real gap in what macOS offers, and BlackHole plus the Multi-Output Device setup is the standard, well-known way people work around that gap on a Mac.

Step 8: Why the Python version had to change
Constraint: Kokoro (the voice model) depends on a specific version of a supporting library that, in turn, needs a specific version of numpy that simply doesn't have a build available for the newest Python version installed on this Mac (3.13). Trying to force it caused the underlying numpy code to fail compiling from scratch. Rather than fight that compatibility gap, we just built the whole project on the older, fully-supported Python 3.12 instead — a small technical workaround, but one entirely forced by a dependency mismatch rather than any real preference for 3.12.

The pattern across almost every decision
Nearly every major design choice in this project traces back to one root cause: running everything locally (no cloud AI, no cloud STT, no cloud TTS) trades away raw speed for privacy, cost, and independence from any external service. Local models are meaningfully slower than cloud ones on this hardware, so the whole system is engineered around minimizing how often the slow part (the LLM) gets called at all — using fast, dumb, predictable code wherever possible, and reserving the actual AI thinking for the handful of moments where nothing else could do the job.

